  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising odds of hydrocarbon supply interruptions push up domestic energy costs and exchange‑rate stress in vulnerable countries, which increases domestic political demands on governments and legislatures to pressure central banks over rates, reserves use, or leadership decisions.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">currency_activism_shocks</data>
</edge>
<edge source="hormuz_standoff::supply_disruption_risk" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_control_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">A rising assessed probability of hydrocarbon export interruptions increases US strategic urgency to limit sensitive technology flows to China (to reduce leverage and constrain military/industrial resilience), prompting tighter and broader export controls.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">hormuz_standoff</data>
