<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::unmanned_weapons_rate" target="ai_governance_crisis::model_autonomy_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">Operational pressure to employ and sustain larger numbers of unmanned systems forces defense and adjacent commercial developers to prioritize, test, and field higher autonomy (navigation, target selection, swarm coordination), raising the average autonomy level of deployed models.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_governance_crisis</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::unmanned_weapons_rate" target="hormuz_standoff::maritime_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Increased operational use and production of loitering munitions and maritime drones in the Russia‑Ukraine war accelerates proliferation and export of those systems (via state and gray‑market channels) to regional actors and proxies who then employ them against shipping in the Strait of Hormuz.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::unmanned_weapons_rate" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::ai_compute_demand" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A sustained rise in deployed unmanned and autonomous systems drives procurement and R&amp;D for autonomy, perception and onboard processing, increasing demand for specialised AI training and edge compute resources.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
