<edge source="currency_activism_shocks::market_volatility_level" target="ai_governance_crisis::regulatory_scrutiny" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A spike in cross‑market volatility tied to currency shocks draws political and supervisory attention to algorithmic and model‑driven risks in financial and critical‑infrastructure firms, prompting faster, broader regulatory inquiries and conditional oversight of AI deployments.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">currency_activism_shocks</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_governance_crisis</data>
</edge>
<edge source="currency_activism_shocks::market_volatility_level" target="hormuz_standoff::oil_price_volatility" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising cross-market volatility drives rapid portfolio rebalancing and risk-premia shifts in commodity desks and hedge funds, producing larger, more frequent swings in crude futures and thus higher oil-price volatility.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">currency_activism_shocks</data>
  <data key="d39">hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="currency_activism_shocks::market_volatility_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::alliance_cohesion_index" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Elevated market volatility raises domestic economic and political pressures in allied countries, prompting some governments to prioritize internal stabilization over coordinated support for Ukraine and thereby weakening alliance cohesion.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">currency_activism_shocks</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
